// The "How it is measured, what drives it, what is done to change it" text on
// every Britain in numbers card, keyed by series id (see onsSectors.js).
//
//   how     where the figure comes from and how it is worked out
//   causes  what typically pushes it up or down
//   levers  what governments, the Bank of England or others usually change to try
//           to move it. Describes the tools, not whether they are a good idea:
//           economists disagree, and each has trade-offs.

const GDP_HOW = "The ONS adds up everything produced in the UK, from surveys of businesses and tax records, and strips out price rises so quarters can be compared. Early estimates are revised as more data arrives.";
const GDP_CAUSES = "Business confidence, consumer spending, exports, interest rates, energy prices, and shocks such as the 2008 financial crisis, the pandemic or a sudden jump in energy costs.";
const GDP_LEVERS = "The Bank of England raises or cuts interest rates to cool or boost spending. The government changes taxes and public spending, and spends on investment, training or planning rules to raise the economy's long-run speed limit.";

const CPI_HOW = "The ONS tracks the prices of around 700 items, a 'basket' of what households typically buy, collected from shops, websites and price scanners. Each item is weighted by how much is spent on it, and the change on a year ago is the headline rate.";
const CPI_LEVERS = "The Bank of England is asked to keep CPI inflation at 2%, and its main tool is the interest rate. The government can change taxes on goods such as fuel, cap energy bills, or subsidise things like childcare and transport fares.";

const UNEMP_LEVERS = "The Bank of England's interest rates and the government's spending and tax decisions affect how many jobs businesses create. Governments also change training, employment support, benefit rules and the minimum wage.";

const HPI_HOW = "HM Land Registry records the price paid for every home sold in England and Wales, and Registers of Scotland and Land & Property Services cover Scotland and Northern Ireland. The ONS and Land Registry combine them into the UK House Price Index, adjusting for the mix of homes sold so that a month with many mansions does not look like a price surge.";
const HPI_CAUSES = "Mortgage interest rates and lending rules, wages, how many homes are built compared with how many people want one, investor demand, and local jobs and transport links.";
const HPI_LEVERS = "Governments change planning rules and house building targets (supply), Stamp Duty and help-to-buy schemes (demand), and landlord taxes. The Bank of England's interest rate and mortgage lending rules also have a large effect.";

const CSEW_HOW = "Each year, interviewers ask tens of thousands of households whether they have been a victim of crime in the past 12 months, then scale the answers up to the whole population. It catches crime that is never reported, but it covers only households (not businesses) and people aged 16 and over, and it is an estimate with a margin of error.";
const PRC_HOW = "Every police force in England and Wales sends the Home Office the crimes it has recorded, and the ONS publishes the totals. It counts crimes that were reported or discovered and then logged, so it depends on how willing people are to report and on how strictly forces record.";
const PRC_CAUSES_NOTE = "Recorded numbers can rise because more crime happens, because more people report it, or because police record it more carefully, so a rise is not always bad news.";
const CRIME_LEVERS = "Governments and forces change police numbers and priorities, sentencing and prison capacity, rules on offences and powers, support for victims, and the things that reduce opportunities for crime, such as youth services, security and design.";

const POP_HOW = "The ONS starts from the Census, then each year adds births, takes off deaths, and adds net migration, using registration records, NHS data and border and visa data. Mid-year estimates describe the population at 30 June.";
const POP_CAUSES = "Births, deaths and migration. Fertility rates, the age of the population, and the number of people arriving or leaving for work, study, family or asylum.";
const POP_LEVERS = "Immigration rules (visas, salary thresholds, student and work routes) are the main lever over short-term change. Family and housing policy, childcare costs and healthcare affect births and deaths more slowly.";

const PRICE_ITEM_HOW = "Part of the same basket the ONS uses for CPI, measuring only the items listed, as a change in price on a year earlier.";

const ENERGY_HOW = "The ONS records what households pay for electricity, gas and heating fuel, using tariff and billing data from suppliers, and works out the change on a year earlier.";
const ENERGY_CAUSES = "Wholesale gas and electricity prices, which follow world markets and events such as the war in Ukraine, the weather, how much power comes from renewables, and the network costs and levies added to bills.";
const ENERGY_LEVERS = "The regulator, Ofgem, sets a price cap on standard tariffs. Governments can cap or subsidise bills, change the taxes and levies on them, and change how electricity is generated through support for renewables, nuclear and gas.";

const RENT_HOW = "The ONS uses rental data from the Valuation Office Agency in England and from equivalent bodies in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland. It tracks how rents change for comparable private rental homes.";
const RENT_CAUSES = "The supply of homes to rent compared with demand from renters, wages, mortgage rates (landlords pass costs on), and the number of landlords leaving or joining the market.";
const RENT_LEVERS = "Governments change the rules on tenancies and rent increases, landlord taxes, and housing benefit rates, and the planning rules that decide how many homes get built. Rent controls are used in some places and strongly debated.";

const EMPLOY_HOW = "The Labour Force Survey asks a sample of households about their work in the past week. The ONS scales the answers up to the whole population. Response rates have fallen in recent years, so the estimates carry more uncertainty than they used to.";

const GHG_HOW = "The ONS adds up emissions by industry and households, using fuel use, industrial processes, farming and waste data, and converts each gas into carbon dioxide equivalents. This is the 'environmental accounts' version, which includes UK residents' flights and shipping abroad.";
const GHG_CAUSES = "How electricity is generated (coal, gas, renewables, nuclear), how much energy people use, transport, heating and industry, the weather, and how much the economy grows.";
const GHG_LEVERS = "Governments set carbon budgets, price carbon (the UK Emissions Trading Scheme), subsidise renewables, ban or phase out things such as coal power and new petrol cars, and set building and efficiency standards.";

const PSF_HOW = "The ONS builds the public sector finances each month from HM Revenue and Customs, Treasury, department and council returns. Monthly figures are not adjusted for the time of year, so this page adds up the latest 12 months to smooth out tax deadlines.";
const TAX_LEVERS = "The Chancellor changes tax rates, thresholds, allowances and reliefs, usually at a Budget. Freezing thresholds while pay rises quietly brings in more tax (fiscal drag), and efforts to cut tax avoidance and evasion add a little more.";

const BANK_HOW = "The Bank of England collects rates from lenders and markets for its statistical database. Figures here are monthly, taken from the Bank's own published series.";
const MORTGAGE_CAUSES = "Bank Rate and what markets expect it to do (which sets lenders' own borrowing costs), competition between lenders, how risky they think borrowers are, and the cost of the money they borrow.";
const MORTGAGE_LEVERS = "The Bank of England's interest rate is the main one, because it feeds through to lenders. The Financial Policy Committee can limit how much people borrow compared with their income, and governments have run schemes that guarantee part of the loan for small deposits.";
const FX_CAUSES = "Interest rates here compared with other countries (higher rates attract money), the UK's trade balance, news about the economy and politics, and the strength of the other currency.";
const FX_LEVERS = "Governments and the Bank of England do not target the pound's value. Interest rate decisions and trade and fiscal policy affect it indirectly, and direct intervention in currency markets is rare.";
const INVEST_HOW = "The ONS asks firms what they spend on machinery, vehicles, software and buildings as part of the quarterly national accounts. Spending on homes and by government is counted separately. The first estimate is revised as more survey returns arrive.";
const INVEST_CAUSES = "How much demand firms expect, profits and cash to spend, interest rates, uncertainty (for example about trade rules or tax changes) and how easy it is to borrow.";
const INVEST_LEVERS = "Tax allowances that let firms deduct what they invest (such as full expensing), the corporation tax rate, public banks and funds that lend to firms, and the Bank of England's interest rate. Stable, predictable rules also help.";

const TRADE_HOW = "The ONS adds up customs data from HM Revenue and Customs for goods and surveys of businesses for services, in current prices and adjusted for the time of year. Recent quarters are revised.";
const IND_HOW = "The ONS builds a monthly index of the volume of output in each industry from surveys of businesses and administrative data, with price rises removed. The latest months are early estimates and are revised.";
const NHS_HOW = "NHS England collects the figures from every hospital trust each month and publishes them for England as a whole. The latest months are provisional and can be revised.";
const NHS_LEVERS = "The government sets the NHS's budget and priorities, and NHS England sets targets for hospitals. In practice the main levers are funding, how many staff are hired and kept, building capacity (beds, scanners, extra operating theatres), using private hospitals for some operations, and reforming how patients flow through hospital and social care.";
const MIG_HOW = "The Office for National Statistics estimates long-term migration, meaning people moving for a year or more, by combining Home Office border and visa records with census and survey data. The latest year is provisional and is revised as more travel records arrive.";
const HO_HOW = "The Home Office publishes these figures every quarter from its own case records. The latest quarter is provisional and can be revised.";
const HO_LEVERS = "Ministers change the immigration rules (who qualifies, for how long, and with what conditions), set the Home Office's budget and staffing, agree arrangements with other countries, and change the law through Parliament. Court rulings can also change what is allowed.";

export const DEEP_DIVES = {
  // --- Economy ---
  "gdp-quarter": { how: GDP_HOW, causes: GDP_CAUSES, levers: GDP_LEVERS },
  "gdp-year": { how: `${GDP_HOW} Here the latest four quarters are compared with the four before.`, causes: GDP_CAUSES, levers: GDP_LEVERS },
  "gdp-head": { how: "GDP is divided by the estimated population. It shows whether the average person's share of the economy is growing, which total GDP can hide when the population is growing quickly.", causes: `${GDP_CAUSES} It also falls when the population grows faster than output, for example through high migration.`, levers: GDP_LEVERS },
  "gdp-level": { how: "The ONS measures GDP in 'chained volume' terms, which values each year's output at the previous year's prices so the figures are comparable across time. The quarterly total is shown in billions of pounds.", causes: "A larger workforce, more capital (machines, buildings, technology), and better productivity. Recessions cause dips, which can take years to recover.", levers: "Investment in infrastructure, skills and research, tax incentives for business investment, trade deals, and planning reform are the usual tools for lifting the long-run size of the economy." },
  "income-head": { how: "Real household disposable income is what households have after tax, benefits and pension contributions, adjusted for inflation and divided by the population. The ONS works it out from the national accounts.", causes: "Wages, employment, tax and benefit rates, interest and dividend income, and prices, which can erode what an income buys.", levers: "Income tax and National Insurance rates and thresholds, benefit and pension rates, the minimum wage, and anything that changes prices all feed into this figure." },
  "productivity": { how: "Total output (GDP) divided by the total hours worked by everyone in the economy. The hours come from the Labour Force Survey and employer surveys.", causes: "How much capital workers have to work with, their skills, technology, management, and how well the economy shifts workers towards more productive firms. It also moves with the economic cycle.", levers: "Governments invest in skills, transport and digital networks, research and development, and tax breaks for business investment. Competition and trade rules matter too. Many economists see productivity as the key to lasting wage growth." },
  "production": { how: "A monthly index built from surveys of manufacturers, power producers, miners and quarry operators, converted to volumes and adjusted for the time of year.", causes: "Demand at home and abroad, energy costs, exchange rates, supply chain problems, and long-run shifts such as the decline of coal and heavy industry.", levers: "Industrial strategy, grants and tax breaks for manufacturing and investment, energy price support, trade agreements and tariffs." },
  "services": { how: "A monthly index built from surveys of firms in services such as retail, finance, health, education and hospitality, converted to volumes and adjusted for the time of year.", causes: "Consumer spending, business demand for professional services, tourism, public spending on health and education, and interest rates.", levers: "Interest rates and household taxes affect spending. Public spending decides how much health and education services are bought. Trade rules affect services exports." },
  "retail": { how: "The ONS Retail Sales Inquiry asks about 5,000 retailers what they sold in a month. Prices are stripped out to give a volume index.", causes: "Wages, employment, consumer confidence, the weather, the timing of Easter and Black Friday, and how much people are saving.", levers: "Income tax, VAT, benefits and interest rates change how much people have to spend. Business rates affect shops' costs." },
  "saving": { how: "Household savings divided by household disposable income, from the national accounts. It counts money put into pensions, savings accounts and investments as well as paying down debt.", causes: "Interest rates (higher rates reward saving), uncertainty and confidence, inflation, and one-off events such as lockdowns when spending was impossible.", levers: "Interest rates, tax-free savings accounts (ISAs), pension rules and auto-enrolment, and how much of income is taxed all affect how much people save." },

  // --- Prices ---
  "cpi": { how: CPI_HOW, causes: "Energy and food prices, the exchange rate (which changes import prices), wages, how much demand there is compared with supply, and one-off shocks such as the war in Ukraine.", levers: CPI_LEVERS },
  "cpih": { how: `${CPI_HOW} CPIH adds the cost of owning and running a home, using rents to estimate what owner-occupiers would pay.`, causes: "The same as CPI, plus rents and the housing costs of owner-occupiers.", levers: CPI_LEVERS },
  "core": { how: `${CPI_HOW} The volatile groups of energy, food, alcohol and tobacco are removed to show the underlying trend.`, causes: "Wages and how much firms pass on costs, domestic demand, and spare capacity in the economy. It is less affected by oil and gas prices than headline inflation.", levers: "The Bank of England watches this closely when deciding on interest rates, because it shows whether higher prices are becoming embedded in the economy." },
  "services-inflation": { how: PRICE_ITEM_HOW, causes: "Services are labour-intensive, so wages are the main driver. Insurance, rents and travel costs also matter.", levers: "Interest rates, the minimum wage and wage policy in the public sector all feed in, as do taxes such as VAT and Insurance Premium Tax." },
  "goods-inflation": { how: PRICE_ITEM_HOW, causes: "Import prices, exchange rates, shipping costs, energy and food prices, and global supply chain problems.", levers: "Trade rules and tariffs, VAT and fuel duty, and measures that change the exchange rate indirectly through interest rates." },
  "food": { how: PRICE_ITEM_HOW, causes: "Weather and harvests, world commodity prices, energy and fertiliser costs, labour costs, transport, and the exchange rate. UK supermarkets compete strongly, which holds some prices down.", levers: "Trade rules and tariffs on food imports, farming subsidies, VAT (most food is zero-rated), and competition rules. Some countries use price caps, which have trade-offs." },
  "energy": { how: ENERGY_HOW, causes: ENERGY_CAUSES, levers: ENERGY_LEVERS },
  "rents": { how: RENT_HOW, causes: RENT_CAUSES, levers: RENT_LEVERS },
  "transport": { how: PRICE_ITEM_HOW, causes: "Oil prices and petrol and diesel costs, car prices and insurance, airfares, and rail and bus fares set by operators and regulators.", levers: "Fuel duty, rail fare caps, bus subsidies and VAT or tax changes on vehicles, including incentives for electric cars." },
  "eating-out": { how: PRICE_ITEM_HOW, causes: "Staff wages (the biggest cost), food and energy bills, rents and business rates, and VAT.", levers: "VAT on hospitality, business rates, the minimum wage and employer National Insurance all change what venues charge." },

  // --- Jobs and pay ---
  "unemployment": { how: `${EMPLOY_HOW} Someone counts as unemployed if they are out of work, looked for work in the last four weeks and could start within two weeks. It uses an international definition, so it is not the same as claiming benefits.`, causes: "The state of the economy and business confidence, interest rates, major shocks such as recessions, and the match between the skills people have and the jobs on offer.", levers: UNEMP_LEVERS },
  "youth-unemployment": { how: `${EMPLOY_HOW} The figure is for 16 to 24 year olds only, and many of them are in full-time education, which is why it can be higher than the overall rate.`, causes: "Young people are often the first to lose jobs in a downturn and the last to be hired. Entry-level jobs, education and training places, and the minimum wage matter.", levers: "Apprenticeships and training schemes, support such as Kickstart, changes to education and school leaving ages, and the youth rate of the minimum wage." },
  "employment": { how: EMPLOY_HOW, causes: "The strength of the economy, the number of people able to work (health and caring), childcare costs, retirement age and how well the benefit system rewards work.", levers: "Childcare support, tax and benefit design so that work pays, back-to-work help, health support and changes to the State Pension age." },
  "employed": { how: EMPLOY_HOW, causes: "How many jobs firms create, the size of the working-age population, migration and how many people choose to work or retire.", levers: "The economy as a whole, plus policies that raise participation and immigration rules." },
  "unemployed": { how: EMPLOY_HOW, causes: "Recessions and slowdowns, redundancies, and changes in how many people look for work.", levers: UNEMP_LEVERS },
  "inactivity": { how: `${EMPLOY_HOW} People are counted as inactive if they are not working and have not looked for work in the past month, or could not start soon.`, causes: "Long-term sickness, caring for family, studying, early retirement and the cost of childcare. An ageing population also raises it.", levers: "NHS waiting times, health and disability support, childcare funding and the design of benefits and work coaching." },
  "redundancy": { how: "The Labour Force Survey asks people if they have been made redundant in the last three months. The figure is the number per 1,000 employees.", causes: "Firms cutting costs in a downturn, closures and restructuring, and sector shocks such as steelworks closing or retail shifting online.", levers: "Redundancy pay and notice rules, support for people losing jobs, and schemes like furlough that keep jobs going through a crisis." },
  "vacancies": { how: "The ONS Vacancy Survey asks about 6,000 employers how many jobs they are trying to fill. It counts vacancies open on a set date.", causes: "Firms' confidence and demand, a shortage of workers or skills, and how long it takes to hire. It rises when the economy is hot and falls in a downturn.", levers: "Training and skills funding, immigration rules for workers, and measures that help people move to where the jobs are." },
  "hours": { how: "The Labour Force Survey asks employees how many hours they actually worked in the reference week, including overtime.", causes: "Demand for work, overtime, working-time rules, the growth of flexible and remote working, and the weather and holidays.", levers: "Working time rules, employment rights, and the tax and benefit system's effect on whether extra hours are worth it." },
  "pay-level": { how: "The ONS Average Weekly Earnings survey asks a large sample of employers (around 9,000) for their total pay bill and number of employees.", causes: "Productivity, the balance of workers to jobs, inflation, pay deals and the mix of jobs in the economy.", levers: "The National Living Wage, public sector pay awards, tax and National Insurance, and policies that raise skills and productivity." },
  "pay-growth": { how: "The ONS compares average weekly earnings over the last three months with the same three months a year earlier. It is not adjusted for inflation.", causes: "Competition for workers, expected inflation, productivity, pay rises in the public sector and increases in the minimum wage.", levers: "The minimum wage, public sector pay settlements, and wider policies on skills, tax and employment rules." },
  "pay-real": { how: "Pay growth minus inflation, calculated by the ONS using the Consumer Prices Index, so it tells you whether pay buys more or less than it did.", causes: "Whether pay is rising faster than prices. It falls when inflation jumps, as it did in 2022, and rises when inflation drops.", levers: "Anything that raises pay (the minimum wage, productivity) or lowers inflation (interest rates, energy price support) moves it. Tax and National Insurance change what is left after deductions." },

  // --- Public finances ---
  "debt": { how: "The ONS and the Office for Budget Responsibility total all the government owes (mostly gilts, or bonds, held by investors) minus cash and liquid assets. The total is divided by a year of GDP, so it shows the burden relative to the economy.", causes: "Borrowing in recessions, the cost of interest, one-off shocks such as the financial crisis, the pandemic and the energy support schemes, and the pace of economic growth.", levers: "Governments change tax rates, public spending, and fiscal rules. A growing economy helps, because the same debt is a smaller share of a bigger GDP. The Bank of England's bond purchases and sales also affect the figures." },
  "debt-level": { how: "The ONS adds up everything owed by central government, local government and public corporations, excluding public sector banks, minus cash and liquid assets. Shown in billions of pounds.", causes: "It rises whenever the government borrows more than it pays off, which happens most years. Inflation and interest rates increase the cost of what is owed.", levers: "Running a surplus pays debt down. Selling assets or having the Bank of England hold debt changes the figures. Most governments aim to stabilise debt as a share of GDP rather than eliminate it." },
  "borrowing": { how: "The ONS measures public sector net borrowing as total spending minus total income (taxes, National Insurance, and other receipts) in each month, using data from HMRC, the Treasury and local councils.", causes: "Economic growth (which lifts tax receipts), unemployment (which raises benefits), interest on debt, and the timing of tax payments, which is why some months show a surplus.", levers: "Tax rates and thresholds, public spending, and benefit rules are the main levers. Fiscal events such as the Budget set the path." },
  "borrowing-12m": { how: "The last 12 monthly borrowing figures added together, so seasonal tax payments cancel out. It is close to the figure used when people talk about the 'deficit' for a year.", causes: "The same as monthly borrowing. Recessions, pandemics and energy support raise it, and growth and tax rises reduce it.", levers: "Tax rises, spending cuts and growth are the ways to reduce borrowing. Fiscal rules set by the Chancellor aim to keep it within limits." },
  "deficit": { how: "The current budget deficit is borrowing excluding investment, so it covers day-to-day costs like salaries, benefits and interest. It comes from the ONS public sector finances release.", causes: "Higher spending on benefits, pensions, health and debt interest, or weaker tax receipts.", levers: "Many fiscal rules aim to balance this measure, because borrowing for investment is treated differently from borrowing to pay running costs. Governments change taxes and day-to-day spending." },
  "investment": { how: "The ONS measures public sector net investment: spending on assets that last, minus the depreciation (wear and tear) of existing assets.", causes: "Large projects such as railways and hospitals can make it uneven. Budgets set by governments, and how much of the borrowing allowed under fiscal rules is used for capital rather than running costs.", levers: "Capital budgets, fiscal rules that allow or restrict borrowing for investment, and the setting up of bodies such as the National Wealth Fund." },
  "public-workers": { how: "The ONS surveys public bodies and counts employees in full-time equivalents, covering the civil service, NHS, local government, education, police and armed forces.", causes: "Changes in public spending, such as the cuts after 2010 or rises for the NHS, and shifts in service demand. Reclassifying organisations between public and private also changes it.", levers: "Departmental budgets, recruitment drives such as extra nurses or police officers, and decisions to outsource or bring services in-house." },

  // --- Population ---
  "uk": { how: POP_HOW, causes: POP_CAUSES, levers: POP_LEVERS },
  "uk-growth": { how: `${POP_HOW} The growth rate compares each year's estimate with the previous year's.`, causes: POP_CAUSES, levers: POP_LEVERS },
  "england": { how: POP_HOW, causes: `${POP_CAUSES} It also depends on people moving between the UK's nations.`, levers: POP_LEVERS },
  "scotland": { how: POP_HOW, causes: `${POP_CAUSES} Scotland has had lower birth rates than England and has relied on migration for growth.`, levers: POP_LEVERS },
  "wales": { how: POP_HOW, causes: `${POP_CAUSES} Wales has an older population than the UK average, so deaths now outnumber births.`, levers: POP_LEVERS },
  "ni": { how: POP_HOW, causes: `${POP_CAUSES} Northern Ireland has had higher birth rates than the rest of the UK.`, levers: POP_LEVERS },

  // --- Health ---
  "nhs-staff": { how: "NHS England and the other three UK health bodies publish how many staff they employ. They are counted as full-time equivalents, using payroll records.", causes: "Funding, how many people train as nurses and doctors, pay and working conditions, how many staff leave or retire, and how many come from abroad.", levers: "Training places, pay awards, recruitment from overseas, retention schemes and the NHS workforce plan, all of which are paid for by the NHS budget." },
  "sick-count": { how: "From the Labour Force Survey: working-age people who say they are not working and not looking for work because they have long-term sickness or a disability.", causes: "An ageing workforce, mental health and musculoskeletal conditions, long NHS waiting lists, and the effect of the pandemic. Changes in how people describe their health can matter too.", levers: "NHS capacity and waiting times, workplace health support, and the disability benefits system including work coaching and conditions for claiming." },
  "long-term-sick": { how: "From the Labour Force Survey: of working-age people who are inactive (not working or seeking work), the share who give long-term sickness or disability as their main reason.", causes: "It changes if more people are sick or if fewer are inactive for other reasons such as study or retirement, so it can rise without more people being ill.", levers: "Health services, disability and sickness benefits, and measures to help people with health conditions stay in or return to work." },

  // --- Housing ---
  "hpi-uk": { how: HPI_HOW, causes: HPI_CAUSES, levers: HPI_LEVERS },
  "homes-years-of-pay": {
    how: "The average UK house price from the UK House Price Index, divided by a year of average pay: the ONS's average weekly earnings across the whole economy, before tax, multiplied by 52. Both are for the same month. Average earnings include part-time work, so they are lower than a full-time worker's pay, and the result is higher than the ONS's official affordability ratio, which uses median full-time earnings.",
    causes: "House prices rising faster than pay, which happens when mortgages are cheap, homes are scarce or investors buy more. It falls when interest rates rise and cool prices, or when pay rises faster than prices.",
    levers: "Governments change planning rules and house building targets (supply), Stamp Duty and help-to-buy schemes (demand), and landlord taxes. The Bank of England's interest rate and mortgage lending rules affect what buyers can borrow, and wage policy such as the minimum wage affects the other half of the sum.",
  },
  "price-vs-pay-growth": {
    how: "The UK House Price Index's annual change in average house prices, minus the annual change in average weekly earnings across the whole economy, both for the same month. The result is in percentage points: 3 means house prices grew 3 points faster than pay over the year.",
    causes: "Mortgage rates and lending rules, how many homes are built compared with how many people want one, and how fast firms raise wages. Pay rises after a burst of inflation can pull the gap below zero even when prices are still rising.",
    levers: "Governments change planning rules and house building targets, Stamp Duty and help-to-buy schemes, and wage floors such as the minimum wage. The Bank of England's interest rate and mortgage lending rules shape how much buyers can borrow.",
  },
  "hpi-change": { how: `${HPI_HOW} The annual change compares the latest month with the same month a year earlier.`, causes: HPI_CAUSES, levers: HPI_LEVERS },
  "hpi-england": { how: HPI_HOW, causes: HPI_CAUSES, levers: HPI_LEVERS },
  "hpi-wales": { how: HPI_HOW, causes: `${HPI_CAUSES} Wales has its own Land Transaction Tax instead of Stamp Duty.`, levers: HPI_LEVERS },
  "hpi-scotland": { how: HPI_HOW, causes: `${HPI_CAUSES} Scotland uses a different sales system and its own Land and Buildings Transaction Tax.`, levers: HPI_LEVERS },
  "hpi-ni": { how: HPI_HOW, causes: HPI_CAUSES, levers: HPI_LEVERS },
  "hpi-london": { how: HPI_HOW, causes: `${HPI_CAUSES} London is affected by international demand, high salaries in finance and professional services, and a shortage of building land.`, levers: HPI_LEVERS },
  "hpi-north-east": { how: HPI_HOW, causes: `${HPI_CAUSES} Lower local wages and slower job growth than the South keep prices lower.`, levers: HPI_LEVERS },
  "hpi-detached": { how: HPI_HOW, causes: `${HPI_CAUSES} Detached houses are bought more by wealthier buyers and are less sensitive to first-time buyer rules.`, levers: HPI_LEVERS },
  "hpi-flat": { how: HPI_HOW, causes: `${HPI_CAUSES} Flats are affected by leasehold rules, service charges and building safety costs.`, levers: HPI_LEVERS },
  "rents-rate": { how: RENT_HOW, causes: RENT_CAUSES, levers: RENT_LEVERS },
  "rents-index": { how: RENT_HOW, causes: RENT_CAUSES, levers: RENT_LEVERS },
  "home-energy": { how: ENERGY_HOW, causes: ENERGY_CAUSES, levers: ENERGY_LEVERS },

  // --- Crime ---
  "csew-all": { how: CSEW_HOW, causes: "Opportunities and motives for crime, security (such as better locks and cars that are harder to steal), policing, the economy, and shifts to online crime that this count leaves out.", levers: CRIME_LEVERS },
  "csew-violence": { how: CSEW_HOW, causes: "Alcohol and the night-time economy, domestic abuse, gang activity, and how safe public places are.", levers: CRIME_LEVERS },
  "csew-theft": { how: CSEW_HOW, causes: "What there is to steal and how well it is protected, how many homes are left empty during the day, and policing.", levers: CRIME_LEVERS },
  "csew-fraud": { how: `${CSEW_HOW} Fraud questions were added in 2017, so there is a shorter history.`, causes: "The shift of life and shopping online, new scam methods, and how well banks and platforms stop fraud.", levers: "Bank and platform rules (such as reimbursing victims), police and National Crime Agency resources, public awareness, and international cooperation against fraud networks." },
  "prc-all": { how: PRC_HOW, causes: `Real changes in crime, plus reporting and recording. ${PRC_CAUSES_NOTE}`, levers: CRIME_LEVERS },
  "prc-violence": { how: PRC_HOW, causes: `Alcohol, domestic abuse, gangs and disorder. ${PRC_CAUSES_NOTE} Police recording of violence tightened from 2014, which raised the totals.`, levers: CRIME_LEVERS },
  "prc-homicide": { how: "Police record every death treated as murder, manslaughter or infanticide. Homicide is the most reliably counted crime, because a body is hard to hide and every case is investigated.", causes: "Domestic violence, drug and gang disputes, knife and gun availability, and mental illness. Single events with many victims can distort a year.", levers: "Violence reduction units, knife and weapon laws, action on gangs and drugs, domestic abuse services and prison and probation policy." },
  "prc-knife": { how: PRC_HOW, causes: `Because this offence is found by police, more stop and search and more patrols raise it. ${PRC_CAUSES_NOTE}`, levers: "Stop and search powers, knife sale and carrying laws, youth programmes and school policing, and sentencing." },
  "prc-sexual": { how: PRC_HOW, causes: `Most sexual offences are never reported, so changes often reflect willingness to come forward and how the police treat reports, rather than the amount of offending. ${PRC_CAUSES_NOTE}`, levers: "Support for victims, police and court capacity, changes in the definition of offences, and public awareness campaigns." },
  "prc-robbery": { how: PRC_HOW, causes: "Opportunity (more people carrying phones and valuables), gangs, drug markets and local policing.", levers: CRIME_LEVERS },
  "prc-burglary": { how: PRC_HOW, causes: "Better home security has pushed it down over decades. Opportunity and the value of what can be stolen also matter.", levers: "Security and target-hardening, police detection, and sentencing for repeat offenders." },
  "prc-theft": { how: PRC_HOW, causes: "Opportunity, the cost of living, the value of goods that can be resold, and shop security.", levers: CRIME_LEVERS },
  "prc-shoplifting": { how: PRC_HOW, causes: "The cost of living, organised theft gangs, and whether shops report incidents or police respond. Self-checkouts and fewer staff raise opportunities.", levers: "Police response times, sentencing rules, retail security, and changes to rules on low-value theft." },
  "prc-damage": { how: PRC_HOW, causes: "Antisocial behaviour, disputes between neighbours, alcohol, and opportunity in public spaces.", levers: "Local policing and community safety teams, youth activities, and the way councils design and manage public spaces." },
  "prc-drugs": { how: PRC_HOW, causes: "This figure is driven heavily by police activity: drugs are only recorded when police find them, so more stop and search or targeted operations raise it.", levers: "Drug laws and enforcement priorities (for example, cannabis warnings), treatment and diversion services, and border and supply-chain policing." },

  // --- Trade ---
  "exports": { how: "The ONS puts together customs data (from HMRC) for goods and surveys of businesses for services, then adds them to get total exports in current prices.", causes: "Demand in other countries, the pound's exchange rate, the trade deals the UK has, and how competitive UK firms are.", levers: "Trade agreements, export finance and promotion, exchange rates (through interest rates), and investment to raise UK firms' competitiveness." },
  "imports": { how: "The ONS puts together customs data (from HMRC) for goods and surveys of businesses for services, then adds them to get total imports in current prices.", causes: "UK demand for foreign goods and energy, the pound's exchange rate, world prices, and trade rules.", levers: "Tariffs and trade rules, border checks, and policies that encourage domestic production of what would otherwise be imported." },
  "trade-balance": { how: "Exports minus imports, both measured by the ONS. The UK has run a deficit in goods for decades, partly offset by a surplus in services.", causes: "How much the UK spends compared with how much it produces, the strength of the pound and the strength of other economies.", levers: "Trade deals, support for exporters, the exchange rate and policies that change how much energy and goods are imported." },
  "current-account": { how: "The ONS adds trade in goods and services, income from investments abroad, and transfers such as aid. The total is divided by GDP.", causes: "The trade balance, but also the profits foreign firms earn in the UK and the UK earns abroad, and flows such as foreign aid.", levers: "Anything that changes saving or investment in the UK, or its competitiveness. A deficit has to be financed by foreign investment or borrowing, which is why it is watched." },

  // --- Environment ---
  "ghg": { how: GHG_HOW, causes: GHG_CAUSES, levers: GHG_LEVERS },
  "co2": { how: GHG_HOW, causes: GHG_CAUSES, levers: GHG_LEVERS },
  "energy-use": { how: "The Department for Energy Security and Net Zero adds up the energy used by homes, transport, industry and services from every fuel, in millions of tonnes of oil equivalent, which puts coal, oil, gas and electricity on the same scale.", causes: "The weather (a colder winter increases heating), industry, population, how efficient homes, cars and appliances are, and energy prices.", levers: "Building and appliance standards, insulation grants, fuel efficiency rules and energy prices through tax or regulation." },
  "non-fossil": { how: "The Department for Energy Security and Net Zero adds the energy from nuclear, wind, solar, hydro and biomass, in millions of tonnes of oil equivalent.", causes: "The number of wind farms and solar panels built, nuclear plants opening or closing, the weather (wind speed, sunshine and rainfall), and the cost of fossil alternatives.", levers: "Subsidy schemes such as Contracts for Difference, planning permission for wind and nuclear, grid connection rules and carbon pricing." },
  "non-fossil-share": { how: "Non-fossil energy divided by total energy used, worked out from the two series above.", causes: "It rises when more clean energy is built or when total energy use falls, and drops in a windless year.", levers: "The same as for non-fossil energy, plus efficiency policies that reduce total use." },

  // --- Economy: investment ---
  "investment-business": { how: INVEST_HOW, causes: INVEST_CAUSES, levers: INVEST_LEVERS },
  "investment-business-growth": { how: `${INVEST_HOW} The growth rate compares each quarter with the same quarter a year earlier.`, causes: INVEST_CAUSES, levers: INVEST_LEVERS },
  "investment-share": { how: "Business investment at the prices of the time, divided by GDP at the prices of the time, for the same quarter. Both come from the ONS national accounts.", causes: INVEST_CAUSES, levers: INVEST_LEVERS },

  // --- Taxes and spending ---
  "receipts-12m": { how: PSF_HOW, causes: "Economic growth, employment and pay, inflation (which pushes up prices, pay and so the tax on them), tax rates and thresholds, and one-off items such as windfall taxes.", levers: TAX_LEVERS },
  "paye-12m": { how: `${PSF_HOW} PAYE is the income tax employers take from pay each month and pass to HMRC.`, causes: "How many people work, how fast pay grows, and whether tax thresholds rise with pay. Frozen thresholds pull more people into paying, and into higher rates.", levers: "The rates and bands of income tax, the personal allowance and the thresholds, all set by the Chancellor. Scotland sets its own rates and bands for its residents." },
  "sa-12m": { how: `${PSF_HOW} Self-assessment is paid on tax returns, mainly in January and July.`, causes: "Profits of the self-employed and landlords, income from dividends and savings, and investment gains. It swings with what people earned a year or more earlier.", levers: "Rates on dividends, savings and rental income, allowances such as the dividend and savings allowances, and compliance schemes such as Making Tax Digital." },
  "nics-12m": { how: `${PSF_HOW} National Insurance is paid by employees, employers and the self-employed.`, causes: "The number of people in work, pay growth, and the rates and thresholds. Employers' contributions rise when firms hire more or pay more.", levers: "The rates and thresholds for employees, employers and the self-employed, and reliefs such as the Employment Allowance. Governments have both cut employee rates and raised employer rates in recent years." },
  "vat-4q": { how: "The ONS takes VAT collected by HM Revenue and Customs from the national accounts, quarter by quarter, and this page adds up the latest four quarters.", causes: "How much people and businesses spend, prices (VAT is a percentage of price), and the mix of what is bought, since some goods are zero-rated or taxed at a lower rate.", levers: "The standard rate (20%), the list of zero-rated and reduced-rate goods, and the registration threshold at which small firms must charge VAT." },
  "corp-4q": { how: "The ONS takes corporation tax from the national accounts, quarter by quarter, and this page adds up the latest four quarters. It is recorded when the profits arise, not when the cash is paid.", causes: "Company profits, the main rate (25% for larger profits since April 2023), reliefs for investment and research, and profits from the North Sea.", levers: "The main and small-profits rates, allowances for investment such as full expensing, research and development reliefs, and one-off levies on particular sectors." },
  "fuel-12m": { how: `${PSF_HOW} Fuel duty is charged per litre of petrol and diesel.`, causes: "How many miles are driven, how efficient vehicles are, and the shift to electric cars. The rate itself has been frozen or cut for many years, so the total falls as fuel use does.", levers: "The rate per litre, whether it is frozen or raised each year, and whether to tax electric vehicles by distance to replace the lost revenue." },
  "council-tax": { how: "The ONS records council tax collected by local councils each calendar year, from the annual national accounts, so it runs a year or more behind the monthly figures.", causes: "The number of homes, the level each council sets, how much it can raise without a local vote, and how much is collected. Scotland and Wales run their own systems.", levers: "The limit on yearly rises before a local referendum is needed, how homes are banded, and support for people on low incomes. Valuations in England date from 1991, which many want updated." },
  "spending-12m": { how: "Total managed expenditure is all public spending on services, benefits and investment. The ONS builds it from department, council and public corporation returns and adds up 12 months.", causes: "Public sector pay, the number of people needing services, benefit and pension levels, inflation, interest on debt, and one-offs such as pandemic support and energy bill help.", levers: "Spending reviews set each department's budget for several years. Governments also decide pay awards, benefit rates, and fiscal rules that limit how much can be borrowed." },
  "benefits-12m": { how: "The ONS measures net social benefits paid, covering the State Pension, Universal Credit, disability benefits, child benefit and more, from payments by the Department for Work and Pensions and others. This page adds up 12 months.", causes: "An ageing population, the number of people claiming (which rises when unemployment or ill-health does), and the yearly uprating of benefits and pensions with prices or pay.", levers: "How benefits and pensions are uprated (for example the triple lock), who is eligible, the State Pension age, the benefit cap, and the work conditions attached to claims." },
  "interest-12m": { how: "The ONS records interest paid on government bonds and other debt. Index-linked bonds add the rise in the retail price index to what is owed, so interest costs jump when inflation does. This page adds up 12 months.", causes: "How much debt there is, the level of interest rates, inflation (because many bonds are index-linked), and how much debt must be refinanced at today's rates.", levers: "Borrowing less, so debt grows more slowly, and the choice of bonds the Debt Management Office sells. The Bank of England's interest rate also feeds through to the cost of new borrowing." },
  "income-tax-share": { how: "PAYE income tax over 12 months divided by everything the public sector received over the same 12 months, both from the ONS public sector finances.", causes: "Pay growth and employment, which lift PAYE faster than other taxes, and changes to other taxes that shrink or grow the total.", levers: TAX_LEVERS },
  "interest-share": { how: "Debt interest over 12 months divided by everything the public sector received over the same 12 months, both from the ONS public sector finances.", causes: "The size of the debt, interest rates, inflation (through index-linked bonds) and how fast tax receipts grow.", levers: "Borrowing less, issuing different kinds of debt, and raising receipts. A fall in interest rates helps most, but is set by the Bank of England, not the government." },
  "benefits-share": { how: "Net social benefits over 12 months divided by total managed expenditure over the same 12 months, both from the ONS public sector finances.", causes: "Population ageing, claims linked to health and unemployment, how benefits are uprated, and changes in other spending that alter the total.", levers: "Benefit and pension rules, the State Pension age and the other spending decisions that change the total." },

  // --- Interest rates and the pound ---
  "bank-rate": { how: "The Bank of England's Monetary Policy Committee votes on the rate around eight times a year. It is the rate the Bank pays commercial banks on money they hold with it. The figure here is the rate on the last day of each month.", causes: "The Bank sets it to keep inflation near 2%. It raises the rate when inflation is too high or the economy is overheating, and cuts it when inflation is low or the economy is weak.", levers: "The nine members of the Monetary Policy Committee vote on it. The Bank can also buy or sell government bonds (quantitative easing and tightening) and give guidance about what it will do next." },
  "mortgage-2y": { how: `${BANK_HOW} It is the average rate on new two-year fixed-rate mortgages for a borrower with a 25% deposit.`, causes: MORTGAGE_CAUSES, levers: MORTGAGE_LEVERS },
  "mortgage-5y": { how: `${BANK_HOW} It is the average rate on new five-year fixed-rate mortgages for a borrower with a 25% deposit.`, causes: MORTGAGE_CAUSES, levers: MORTGAGE_LEVERS },
  "mortgage-2y-90": { how: `${BANK_HOW} It is the average rate on new two-year fixed-rate mortgages for a borrower with only a 10% deposit.`, causes: `${MORTGAGE_CAUSES} Borrowers with small deposits are riskier, so lenders charge them more.`, levers: MORTGAGE_LEVERS },
  "credit-card": { how: `${BANK_HOW} It is the average rate on credit card borrowing for households.`, causes: "Bank Rate, how many borrowers fail to repay, lenders' costs and competition, and the rules lenders must follow.", levers: "The Financial Conduct Authority regulates how lenders treat people who carry debts for a long time. Bank Rate also feeds through, and a cap on the overall cost of credit has been debated but not set." },
  "savings-bond": { how: `${BANK_HOW} It is the average rate paid on new one-year fixed-rate savings bonds.`, causes: "Bank Rate, how much banks want to attract deposits, and competition between banks and building societies.", levers: "Bank Rate, the allowances that let savers earn some interest tax-free, and deposit protection that keeps savers' money safe if a bank fails." },
  "gilt-10y": { how: "The Bank of England estimates the yield from the prices at which government bonds (gilts) trade in the market. It is shown here as the monthly average for the 10-year point on that curve.", causes: "What markets expect for Bank Rate and inflation, how much the government is borrowing, how much investors want to hold UK debt, and yields around the world.", levers: "The Debt Management Office decides how many bonds to sell and of what length. The Chancellor's plans and the Office for Budget Responsibility's forecasts shape confidence, and the Bank of England can buy or sell bonds." },
  "usd": { how: "The Bank of England records the spot rate at which pounds are exchanged for US dollars in the market. The figure here is the average for the month.", causes: FX_CAUSES, levers: FX_LEVERS },
  "eur": { how: "The Bank of England records the spot rate at which pounds are exchanged for euros in the market. The figure here is the average for the month.", causes: FX_CAUSES, levers: FX_LEVERS },
  "sterling-index": { how: "The Bank of England combines the pound's value against many currencies, weighting each by how much the UK trades with that country, into one index set to 100 in January 2005. The figure here is the monthly average.", causes: FX_CAUSES, levers: FX_LEVERS },
  "mortgage-approvals": { how: "The Bank of England collects the number of new mortgages banks and building societies approve each month, and adjusts the figure for the time of year. An approval comes before the sale completes.", causes: "Mortgage rates, house prices, confidence and job security, how much lenders will lend, and changes to taxes on buying a home.", levers: "Bank Rate, the Financial Policy Committee's limits on high-income-multiple lending, stamp duty changes and government guarantees for small deposits." },
  "remortgage-approvals": { how: "The Bank of England collects the number of mortgages approved to people switching to a new deal on a home they already own, adjusted for the time of year.", causes: "How many fixed deals are ending, the gap between the old and the new rate, and how easy it is to switch.", levers: "Bank Rate and the rules lenders follow when existing customers want a new deal, including the Mortgage Charter agreed between lenders and the government." },

  // --- Health: waiting times ---
  "rtt-waiting": { how: `${NHS_HOW} A 'pathway' is one wait for treatment, so someone waiting for two different treatments counts twice.`, causes: "More people needing care as the population ages, staff shortages, too few beds and operating theatres, industrial action, and patients who cannot leave hospital because social care is not there. Waits that built up during the pandemic have been slow to clear.", levers: NHS_LEVERS },
  "rtt-within-18": { how: `${NHS_HOW} The share is worked out for everyone still waiting on the last day of the month.`, causes: "The same things that lengthen the waiting list: too little capacity for the number of patients, staffing, and delays discharging patients from hospital.", levers: NHS_LEVERS },
  "rtt-over-52": { how: `${NHS_HOW} It counts waits that have already lasted over 52 weeks.`, causes: "A few specialities (such as orthopaedics, ear nose and throat, and gynaecology) with the biggest backlogs, and patients who were hard to schedule during the pandemic.", levers: `${NHS_LEVERS} Hospitals have also been told to focus on the longest waiters first.` },
  "rtt-median": { how: `${NHS_HOW} The median is the wait of the person in the middle of those still waiting.`, causes: "The balance between how many new patients join the list and how many are treated each month.", levers: NHS_LEVERS },
  "ae-4-hour": { how: "Every A&E department and urgent treatment centre in England reports each month how many patients were admitted, transferred or discharged within four hours of arriving. NHS England adds them up. Months from May 2019 to May 2023 leave out some hospitals and cannot be compared exactly.", causes: "Too many patients arriving, and too few beds to admit them into, because patients ready to leave hospital are stuck without social care. Winter illness and staff shortages make it worse.", levers: NHS_LEVERS },
  "ae-4-hour-major": { how: "The same A&E figures, for the full 24-hour hospital departments (type 1) only. They treat the most serious cases, and hold patients longer than minor injury units.", causes: "The same as for the overall four-hour figure, but worse in the departments that take the sickest patients and depend most on free hospital beds.", levers: NHS_LEVERS },
  "cancer-62-day": { how: "NHS England collects the number of patients who began treatment within 62 days of an urgent GP referral for suspected cancer, a screening referral, or an upgrade by a consultant. Figures are published monthly and the latest is provisional.", causes: "Shortages of scanners, pathologists and cancer surgeons, a rise in urgent referrals, and delays between the tests, the diagnosis and the first treatment.", levers: `${NHS_LEVERS} Cancer care has specific levers too: more diagnostic centres, faster test results, and national screening programmes.` },
  "cancer-28-day": { how: "NHS England collects the number of urgently referred patients told within 28 days whether they have cancer, a measure called the Faster Diagnosis Standard. Figures are monthly and the latest is provisional.", causes: "Waits for scans and tests, too few staff to read the results, and patients who need more than one test.", levers: `${NHS_LEVERS} Community diagnostic centres, which do scans away from hospitals, are one of the main tools.` },
  "cancer-31-day": { how: "NHS England collects the number of cancer patients whose first or later treatment began within 31 days of agreeing a treatment plan. Figures are monthly and the latest is provisional.", causes: "Shortages of operating theatres, surgeons and radiotherapy capacity.", levers: `${NHS_LEVERS} Extra radiotherapy machines and surgical hubs are the main tools.` },
  "ambulance-c2": { how: "NHS England collects, from each ambulance service, the time from a 999 call being answered to the first ambulance arriving, and averages it across England for Category 2 calls, which include strokes, heart attacks and serious injuries.", causes: "Ambulances queuing outside A&E because hospitals have no free bed to take the patient, which stops crews getting to the next call, plus staff shortages and rising demand.", levers: `${NHS_LEVERS} Cutting handover delays at hospital doors frees ambulances, and more call handlers and paramedics shorten waits.` },
  "ambulance-c1": { how: "NHS England collects, from each ambulance service, the time from a 999 call being answered to the first ambulance arriving, and averages it across England for Category 1 calls: people in immediate danger, such as a cardiac arrest.", causes: "These calls get the quickest response so the average is steadier, but it still rises when crews are held up outside hospitals.", levers: `${NHS_LEVERS}` },

  // --- Housing supply ---
  "homes-net": { how: "The Ministry of Housing, Communities and Local Government collects, from councils' records, the new homes built, homes created by converting other buildings, and homes lost to demolition or merging, over the financial year to March. The latest year is provisional.", causes: "How profitable it is to build, whether builders can get planning permission, the cost of materials, labour and mortgages, and the number of builders and skilled trades.", levers: "Planning rules and targets (such as mandatory local housing targets), changes to the green belt, public money for social and affordable homes, support for small builders, and the interest rates that set the cost of borrowing to build." },
  "homes-completed-12m": { how: "The Ministry of Housing, Communities and Local Government counts new homes completed each quarter from building inspectors' records. This page adds up the last four quarters. It leaves out conversions, so it is lower than the net additional figure.", causes: "Planning permissions granted two or more years earlier, builders' confidence, mortgage rates, and shortages of workers and materials.", levers: "Planning reform, public funding for affordable homes, and measures to speed up utilities and road connections that hold up sites." },
  "homes-started-12m": { how: "Building control inspectors record when work starts on each new home. The Ministry of Housing, Communities and Local Government adds them up by quarter, and this page adds up the last four quarters.", causes: "How many homes have planning permission, whether builders think they can sell at a profit, interest rates, and nutrient and other rules that delay sites.", levers: "Planning permission rules and speed, incentives for builders to start building, and demand support such as mortgage guarantees." },

  // --- Immigration ---
  "migration-net": { how: MIG_HOW, causes: "Changes in the immigration rules, the numbers of work, study and family visas, international events that cause people to seek protection (such as war in Ukraine or Hong Kong's new rules), and how many people who arrived earlier now leave.", levers: HO_LEVERS },
  "migration-in": { how: MIG_HOW, causes: "Visa rules (especially for health and care workers and students), the strength of the UK job market compared with other countries, and humanitarian schemes.", levers: HO_LEVERS },
  "migration-out": { how: MIG_HOW, causes: "People who came on study or work visas leaving when they end, British people moving abroad, and the size of the groups that arrived in earlier years.", levers: HO_LEVERS },
  "migration-non-eu": { how: MIG_HOW, causes: "Changes to work, study and family visa rules, particularly the opening of the care worker route, and demand from countries such as India, Nigeria and China.", levers: HO_LEVERS },
  "small-boats-month": { how: "The Home Office counts the people detected crossing the English Channel by boat and brought ashore, from operational records. The monthly figure is the daily counts added up. The data is provisional and revised.", causes: "The weather (calm seas allow crossings), smugglers' operations, the number of people trying to reach the UK from France, and French and UK policing of beaches.", levers: "Cooperation with France and other countries to stop boats leaving, funding for border enforcement, new laws on who can claim asylum, safe routes that offer an alternative, and return agreements." },
  "small-boats-12m": { how: "The Home Office counts the people detected crossing the English Channel by boat, from operational records. This page adds up the last 12 months so the effect of the weather evens out.", causes: "The same as for the monthly figure: weather, smugglers' operations, how many people are trying to reach the UK, and policing on both sides of the Channel.", levers: "Cooperation with France and other countries to stop boats leaving, funding for border enforcement, new laws on who can claim asylum, safe routes that offer an alternative, and return agreements." },
  "asylum-claims": { how: HO_HOW, causes: "Conflict and persecution in the countries people come from, how easy it is to reach the UK, and the rules on who can claim.", levers: HO_LEVERS },
  "asylum-awaiting": { how: HO_HOW, causes: "How many claims arrive compared with how many decisions the Home Office makes, which depends on how many caseworkers it employs and how quickly it trains them.", levers: `${HO_LEVERS} Hiring and training more caseworkers, and simpler decision processes, speed up decisions.` },
  "asylum-hotels": { how: HO_HOW, causes: "The size of the asylum backlog and the shortage of other housing for asylum seekers, so people stay in hotels while they wait for a decision.", levers: "Faster decisions, which free up accommodation, plus other housing such as large sites, shared housing and military sites, and rules on who is entitled to support." },
  "asylum-support": { how: HO_HOW, causes: "The number of people waiting for a decision, how long they wait, and how quickly those who are granted protection move on to their own housing.", levers: HO_LEVERS },
  "asylum-grant-rate": { how: HO_HOW, causes: "The nationalities claiming asylum in the period, since some countries' claims are granted far more often than others, and changes to the rules for deciding claims.", levers: HO_LEVERS },
  "returns-enforced": { how: HO_HOW, causes: "How many people are in the UK without the right to stay, return agreements with other countries, detention capacity, and court challenges that delay removals.", levers: "Return agreements with other countries, more staff and detention space, and changes to the law to speed up removals." },
  "returns-voluntary": { how: HO_HOW, causes: "Financial help to leave, people's own decision to go home or on to another country, and the number of people refused permission to stay.", levers: "The size of the financial support offered, and how firmly people are told to leave if they have no right to stay." },
  "visas-work": { how: HO_HOW, causes: "Employer demand for workers, particularly in health and care, the salary thresholds and list of eligible jobs, and the cost of the visa and health surcharge.", levers: HO_LEVERS },
  "visas-study": { how: HO_HOW, causes: "Universities' recruitment, the cost of fees, rules on whether students can bring family, and competition from other countries.", levers: HO_LEVERS },
  "visas-family": { how: HO_HOW, causes: "The number of people with a partner or relative abroad, and the minimum income a sponsor must earn, which was raised in 2024.", levers: HO_LEVERS },

  // --- Prices: more of the basket ---
  "alcohol-tobacco": { how: PRICE_ITEM_HOW, causes: "Duty rises each year (set above inflation for tobacco), the cost of ingredients and energy for brewers and distillers, and pub and shop margins.", levers: "The Chancellor sets alcohol and tobacco duty at each Budget, and governments have also used minimum unit pricing (in Scotland and Wales) and plain packaging to cut smoking and drinking." },
  "clothing": { how: PRICE_ITEM_HOW, causes: "The price of imported goods and so the exchange rate, shipping and cotton costs, wages abroad, and heavy discounting by retailers.", levers: "Mostly outside government control. Trade rules and tariffs matter, and so does the pound, which the Bank of England affects indirectly." },
  "housing-fuels": { how: `${PRICE_ITEM_HOW} It covers private rents, repairs and maintenance, water and sewerage, and gas, electricity and heating oil, but not mortgage costs or house prices.`, causes: "Energy prices set by world markets, rents, and the cost of labour and materials for repairs.", levers: "Energy bills are shaped by the Ofgem price cap and levies on bills. Rents are affected by housing supply and tenancy rules, and water bills by the water regulator Ofwat." },
  "household-goods": { how: PRICE_ITEM_HOW, causes: "Imported goods prices and the pound, shipping costs, and demand for home improvement, which rose in the pandemic and has since cooled.", levers: "Mostly outside government control, apart from trade rules, VAT and the interest rates that affect big purchases." },
  "health-prices": { how: PRICE_ITEM_HOW, causes: "Prices of medicines, charges for dental care and optical services, and the cost of private health services and medical equipment.", levers: "Prescription and dental charges are set by governments in each nation (prescriptions are free in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland, and in England for many groups)." },
  "communication": { how: PRICE_ITEM_HOW, causes: "Annual price rises written into phone and broadband contracts, the cost of building networks, and competition between providers.", levers: "The regulator Ofcom sets rules on how providers raise prices mid-contract, and competition policy affects how many providers there are." },
  "recreation": { how: PRICE_ITEM_HOW, causes: "Demand for holidays and leisure, airfares and fuel costs, wages in the sector, and the pound against other currencies for holidays abroad.", levers: "Mostly set by markets. The government sets VAT and the rates of Air Passenger Duty on flights." },
  "education-prices": { how: PRICE_ITEM_HOW, causes: "Tuition fee levels in England, which are set by the government and have risen in steps, and the cost of nursery and other education paid for by households.", levers: "The government sets the maximum university tuition fee in England, and the devolved governments set their own. Childcare funding changes what households pay." },
  "misc-prices": { how: PRICE_ITEM_HOW, causes: "Insurance premiums, which rise with claims costs (for example the cost of car repairs), plus the price of financial services, hairdressing and personal care.", levers: "The Financial Conduct Authority regulates insurance and financial products. Insurance Premium Tax is set by the Chancellor." },
  "rpi": { how: "The ONS has long published RPI alongside CPI. It uses a different way of averaging prices, which makes it run higher, and it counts some housing costs such as mortgage interest. The UK Statistics Authority no longer treats it as an official statistic.", causes: "The same prices as CPI, but averaged differently, plus mortgage interest payments and council tax, which CPI leaves out.", levers: "The Bank of England targets CPI, not RPI. RPI still sets rail fares, student loan interest, index-linked gilts and some pensions, and the government has announced it will align it with CPIH from 2030." },
  "ppi-output": { how: "The ONS asks manufacturers each month what they charge for goods they sell in the UK, and works out how those prices have changed over the year. It covers goods made in the UK, not services.", causes: "The prices of materials and energy that factories use, the exchange rate, wages and demand.", levers: "Mostly outside government control. Energy price support and trade policy affect it, and so does the Bank of England's interest rate." },

  // --- Jobs: more of how people work ---
  "part-time": { how: `${EMPLOY_HOW} People say for themselves whether their job is full-time or part-time.`, causes: "Caring responsibilities, study, health, and employers offering flexible hours, plus people who would like full-time work but cannot find it.", levers: "Childcare support, flexible working rules and the benefits system's treatment of hours all shape how many people work part-time." },
  "self-employed": { how: `${EMPLOY_HOW} Self-employment is counted from people's own description of their main job.`, causes: "Entrepreneurs, tradespeople and freelancers, plus people who work as contractors because employers hire that way. The pandemic and changes to tax rules for contractors have shifted the numbers.", levers: "Tax and National Insurance for the self-employed, rules on employment status (whether someone counts as an employee), and support such as the Self-Employment Income Support Scheme in the pandemic." },
  "temp-share": { how: `${EMPLOY_HOW} Temporary means the job has a set end date, such as fixed-term, agency and seasonal work.`, causes: "Employers' caution about hiring permanently, seasonal industries, and agencies. The share fell steadily after 2015.", levers: "Employment law on fixed-term and agency work, and rules on the rights of temporary workers." },
  "long-term-unemployed": { how: `${EMPLOY_HOW} It counts people unemployed for a year or more by their own reports of how long they have been looking.`, causes: "The state of the labour market, the skills people have compared with the jobs on offer, health problems, and the time spent out of work, which makes employers less likely to hire.", levers: "Back-to-work support and training, wage subsidies for employers who hire long-term unemployed people, and work requirements linked to benefits." },
  "youth-employment": { how: `${EMPLOY_HOW} It looks only at 16 to 24 year olds who are not in full-time education.`, causes: "The state of the economy, the availability of entry-level jobs and apprenticeships, and the minimum wage for young workers.", levers: "Apprenticeships, work experience and training schemes for young people, the youth rates of the minimum wage and the age at which people can leave education or training." },
  "pay-public": { how: "The ONS Average Weekly Earnings survey asks a large sample of employers (around 9,000) for their pay bill and number of employees, and splits public from private employers.", causes: "Government pay awards for each year, set after advice from independent pay review bodies, and the need to recruit and keep staff in nursing, teaching and other jobs.", levers: "The Treasury sets how much pay can rise and the pay review bodies recommend awards. Strikes and recruitment shortages push the government to settle at higher levels." },
  "pay-private": { how: "The ONS Average Weekly Earnings survey asks a large sample of employers (around 9,000) for their pay bill and number of employees, and splits private from public employers.", causes: "Competition for staff, how well firms are doing, inflation and the minimum wage, which sets the floor for pay in many industries.", levers: "The National Living Wage, employment rights and tax on pay, and policies that raise productivity, which is what pays for lasting increases." },
  "strikes-12m": { how: "The ONS counts working days lost to strikes from employers' reports to the Department for Business and Trade and its own checks. This page adds up the last 12 months.", causes: "Pay that has fallen behind prices, disputes over conditions, and decisions by unions to ballot members. The 2022 and 2023 peak followed a period of high inflation and pay restraint in the public sector.", levers: "Pay awards and talks between unions and employers, the law on strike ballots and minimum service levels, and measures to speed up settling disputes." },

  // --- Economy: spending and investment growth ---
  "government-spending-growth": { how: "The ONS measures how much general government spends on running services such as the NHS, schools and defence, with price rises removed, from public sector accounts. The growth rate compares each quarter with the same quarter a year before.", causes: "Budgets set in spending reviews, public sector pay, demand for services such as the NHS, and one-off spending such as pandemic support.", levers: "Departmental budgets and pay awards, set by the Treasury at spending reviews and the Budget, within the government's fiscal rules." },
  "investment-total-growth": { how: "The ONS measures spending on machinery, buildings, housing and infrastructure by businesses, households and the government from surveys and public accounts, with price rises removed. The growth rate compares each quarter with the same quarter a year before.", causes: "Business confidence and profits, interest rates, house-building, and public investment in roads, railways and hospitals.", levers: "Tax allowances for investment, public capital budgets, planning reform to speed up building, and the Bank of England's interest rate." },

  // --- Trade ---
  "goods-exports": { how: TRADE_HOW, causes: "Demand in other countries, the pound's exchange rate, the UK's trade deals and customs rules, and how competitive UK manufacturers are.", levers: "Trade agreements, export finance and promotion, customs and border rules, and support for manufacturers." },
  "goods-imports": { how: TRADE_HOW, causes: "UK demand for foreign goods and energy, the pound's exchange rate, and world prices for oil, gas and food.", levers: "Tariffs, customs and border checks, and policies that encourage making more goods in the UK." },
  "goods-balance": { how: `${TRADE_HOW} It is exports of goods minus imports of goods.`, causes: "The UK buys more goods than it makes, partly because it has specialised in services and partly because of demand for cars, electronics and fuel.", levers: "Trade policy and industrial policy to make more goods at home, and the exchange rate, which the Bank of England affects through interest rates." },
  "services-exports": { how: TRADE_HOW, causes: "The strength of UK finance, law, consultancy, education and the creative industries, plus the pound and rules on selling services into other markets.", levers: "Trade agreements that cover services, regulation of financial services, visas for students and visitors, and support for the creative industries." },
  "services-imports": { how: TRADE_HOW, causes: "UK residents' holidays abroad, cloud and software services bought from overseas, shipping and insurance, and the pound.", levers: "Mostly set by what people and firms choose to buy. Tax on foreign digital services and flight taxes affect it a little." },
  "services-balance": { how: `${TRADE_HOW} It is exports of services minus imports of services.`, causes: "The UK sells far more services than it buys, because of its strength in finance, law and education.", levers: "Trade agreements and regulation that make it easier to sell UK services abroad." },
  "eu-balance": { how: `${TRADE_HOW} It is goods sold to EU countries minus goods bought from them.`, causes: "UK demand for EU cars, food and machinery, and EU demand for UK exports, both shaped by the trade rules since Brexit and the pound.", levers: "The terms of the UK-EU trade agreement and any changes to customs checks, standards or regulatory cooperation." },
  "noneu-balance": { how: `${TRADE_HOW} It is goods sold to countries outside the EU minus goods bought from them.`, causes: "UK demand for goods and fuel from the US, China, Norway and other countries, and their demand for UK exports.", levers: "Trade deals with countries outside the EU, such as those with Australia, India and the Pacific group, and tariffs on both sides." },
  "eu-export-share": { how: `${TRADE_HOW} It is goods exports to the EU as a share of all goods exports.`, causes: "The size of the EU market, its closeness, and the trade rules after Brexit, plus growth in sales to the US and Asia.", levers: "Trade agreements with the EU and with other countries decide how much of UK trade goes where." },

  // --- Housing: types of home and sales ---
  "hpi-semi": { how: HPI_HOW, causes: HPI_CAUSES, levers: HPI_LEVERS },
  "hpi-terraced": { how: HPI_HOW, causes: HPI_CAUSES, levers: HPI_LEVERS },
  "hpi-new-build": { how: HPI_HOW, causes: `${HPI_CAUSES} New-build prices also depend on the mix of homes completed and on builders' costs and incentives.`, levers: HPI_LEVERS },
  "hpi-existing": { how: HPI_HOW, causes: HPI_CAUSES, levers: HPI_LEVERS },
  "hpi-sales": { how: "HM Land Registry counts sales as they are registered, which happens weeks after the sale completes, so the latest two months are not yet available.", causes: "Mortgage rates and lenders' willingness to lend, buyers' confidence, tax changes such as Stamp Duty deadlines (which bring sales forward), and how many homes are for sale.", levers: "Stamp Duty and other taxes on buying and selling, mortgage rules and guarantees, and Bank Rate, which sets the cost of borrowing." },

  // --- Crime ---
  "csew-injury": { how: CSEW_HOW, causes: "Alcohol and the night-time economy, domestic abuse, and disputes between people who know each other. Most violence is not committed by strangers.", levers: CRIME_LEVERS },
  "csew-burglary": { how: CSEW_HOW, causes: "Security (locks, alarms, cameras), the number of homes left empty in the day, and the value of what can be sold quickly, such as phones and laptops.", levers: CRIME_LEVERS },
  "csew-vehicle": { how: CSEW_HOW, causes: "How easy cars are to steal (immobilisers made them harder, keyless entry made some easier), and the rise of thefts of parts.", levers: CRIME_LEVERS },
  "csew-damage": { how: CSEW_HOW, causes: "Disputes between neighbours, antisocial behaviour, alcohol and vandalism, which is more common where public spaces are poorly lit or looked after.", levers: CRIME_LEVERS },
  "csew-person-theft": { how: CSEW_HOW, causes: "How many people carry phones and cards, how crowded places are, and organised phone-snatching in big cities.", levers: CRIME_LEVERS },
  "prc-injury": { how: PRC_HOW, causes: `Real changes in violence, plus how carefully police record it. ${PRC_CAUSES_NOTE}`, levers: CRIME_LEVERS },
  "prc-stalking": { how: PRC_HOW, causes: `Stalking and harassment were made separate offences, which counted more cases, and more people now come forward. ${PRC_CAUSES_NOTE}`, levers: CRIME_LEVERS },
  "prc-rape": { how: PRC_HOW, causes: `Most rapes are not reported, so changes reflect willingness to report and police recording, as well as how many happen. ${PRC_CAUSES_NOTE}`, levers: "Support for victims, police and court capacity, how prosecutions are handled, and public awareness campaigns." },
  "prc-vehicle": { how: PRC_HOW, causes: "New methods of stealing vehicles and parts, how easy they are to sell, and how well they are secured.", levers: CRIME_LEVERS },
  "prc-person-theft": { how: PRC_HOW, causes: "Phone snatching in big cities, crowded places and events, and willingness to report a stolen phone, which is needed for an insurance claim.", levers: CRIME_LEVERS },
  "prc-bicycle": { how: PRC_HOW, causes: "More people cycling, how well bikes are locked, and the low chance of getting a stolen bike back, which discourages reporting.", levers: CRIME_LEVERS },
  "prc-public-order": { how: PRC_HOW, causes: "Police activity and decisions to charge, plus real changes in disorder and abuse. Offences rose after the law and recording changed.", levers: CRIME_LEVERS },
  "prc-weapons": { how: PRC_HOW, causes: "Police stop and search, which finds weapons, as well as how many people actually carry them.", levers: "Stop and search powers, weapon laws, school and youth programmes, and sentencing." },

  // --- Environment: emissions by source ---
  "ghg-households": { how: GHG_HOW, causes: "Gas boilers and other heating, the cost of switching to heat pumps, petrol and diesel cars, and how well homes are insulated.", levers: "Standards for new homes and boilers, grants for heat pumps and insulation, the phase-out of new petrol and diesel cars, and taxes on fuels." },
  "ghg-power": { how: GHG_HOW, causes: "The closure of coal power stations, the growth of wind and solar, and how much gas is burned when the wind is low and demand is high.", levers: "Subsidies for renewables and nuclear, the carbon price in the Emissions Trading Scheme, grid connection rules, and the target for clean power." },
  "ghg-manufacturing": { how: GHG_HOW, causes: "The size of heavy industry such as steel, chemicals and cement, the energy they use, and the offshoring of production to countries with lower costs.", levers: "The carbon price, support for switching to hydrogen and electricity, and rules on products imported from countries with weaker climate policies." },
  "ghg-agriculture": { how: GHG_HOW, causes: "Livestock numbers, which release methane, fertiliser use, which releases nitrous oxide, and the fuel used on farms.", levers: "Farm payments linked to environmental practice, support for lower-emission techniques, and public diets, which affect how much meat and dairy is produced." },
  "ghg-transport": { how: GHG_HOW, causes: "Freight, buses, flights and shipping run by businesses, and the cost and availability of cleaner vehicles and fuels.", levers: "Targets for zero-emission lorries and buses, aviation fuel standards and taxes, and rail investment." },

  // --- Business and industry ---
  "manufacturing-growth": { how: IND_HOW, causes: "Demand at home and abroad, the cost of energy and materials, the pound, and long-run shifts away from heavy industry.", levers: "Industrial strategy, support for investment in factories, energy costs, and trade rules." },
  "construction-growth": { how: IND_HOW, causes: "Interest rates, how many homes and offices are being started, public spending on infrastructure, and costs and shortages of materials and workers.", levers: "Planning permission and housing targets, public capital spending, and mortgage and interest rate policy." },
  "retail-trade-growth": { how: IND_HOW, causes: "Household incomes and confidence, the weather, the shift to online shopping, and the timing of events such as Black Friday.", levers: "Income tax, VAT and benefits that affect spending, business rates on shops, and the Bank of England's interest rate." },
  "hospitality-growth": { how: IND_HOW, causes: "Household budgets, tourism, staff costs and shortages, energy bills and the VAT rate on hospitality.", levers: "VAT on food and drink, business rates, the minimum wage and visa rules for overseas workers." },
  "finance-growth": { how: IND_HOW, causes: "Activity in financial markets, interest rates, lending and the competitiveness of London as a financial centre.", levers: "Financial regulation, the rules on how UK firms can sell services abroad, and tax on banks." },
  "tech-comms-growth": { how: IND_HOW, causes: "Business and household spending on software, data and telecoms, and investment in networks and artificial intelligence.", levers: "Support for digital infrastructure and research, competition rules and skills policy." },
  "health-social-growth": { how: IND_HOW, causes: "NHS and social care budgets, staff numbers, and demand from an ageing population.", levers: "Public spending decisions on the NHS and social care, and policies on staffing and pay." },
  "transport-storage-growth": { how: IND_HOW, causes: "Trade and shopping, fuel prices, how many people travel, and changes at borders and ports.", levers: "Investment in roads, railways and ports, fuel duty, and rules on aviation and shipping." },
  "agriculture-growth": { how: IND_HOW, causes: "Weather and harvests, disease in livestock, world food prices and the cost of fertiliser and fuel.", levers: "Farm subsidies and the payments schemes that replaced EU farm payments, trade rules for food and animal health measures." },
  "online-share": { how: "The ONS Retail Sales Inquiry asks retailers what share of their sales was made online and adds up the answers.", causes: "Faster broadband and smartphones, the pandemic, which pushed many people online for good, and shops' own websites and delivery services.", levers: "Business rates, which weigh on shops more than on warehouses, tax rules for online sellers and planning rules for high streets." },
  "online-sales": { how: "The ONS Retail Sales Inquiry asks retailers what they sold online and adds up the answers, shown as an average per week in the month.", causes: "Household spending, the growth of online shopping, and prices.", levers: "Business rates, tax rules for online sellers and consumer protection rules." },
};
